# K1 / B008 — Run 2 replica annotation (blind).

C R176 OL GOV-017,GOV-005,DEP-002 | MUST | Classify a decision with the Reserved list and five-question test from CLAUDE.md: Delegated proceeds without the decision workflow; Decided follows the existing record, and reopening a Decided decision is itself Reserved. | Any decision during work | none stated
C R177 OL GOV-017,ARC-020,GOV-018 | MUST (Reserved: stop affected work) | For a Reserved choice present a Decision Request (title, context, 2-3 realistic options with trade-offs/reversibility/cost, recommendation citing the governing policy, Blocked work, Proceeding-with compliant work) and stop the affected work until the user answers; compliant non-Reserved work may continue. | Reserved decisions | Compliant non-Reserved work may proceed meanwhile.
C R178 OL GOV-017 | MUST | Headless or autonomous runs follow CLAUDE.md decision control and must not silently take Reserved decisions; the Decision Request's Proceeding-with field names the compliant path actually taken. | Headless/autonomous runs | none stated
C R179 OL GOV-017,GOV-007 | MUST | After approval, record the accepted decision as a row of the decision register's Accepted decisions table (date, decision, scope, record link) and update the affected register section (stack, conditional technology, project default or capability selection). | Approved decisions | none stated
C X32 EX GOV-017,GOV-007 | MUST (conditional) | For a consequential or non-obvious accepted decision, add a docs/decisions/NNNN-short-title.md record (context, decision, alternatives considered, consequences, date, approver). | Consequential or non-obvious approved decisions | none stated | GOV-017 requires approval of Reserved changes and GOV-007 synchronization of approved product rules, but no oracle policy requires a separate architecture decision record with fixed content. | Approving e.g. a new persistence provider obliges the agent to write a numbered decision record with alternatives and consequences.
C X33 EX GOV-017,WF-009 | MUST | The architecture-decision workflow reports the decision, its class, the approval source, register/record updates and any request still unresolved. | End of an architecture-decision run | none stated | Reporting duty specific to the decision workflow; WF-009 covers verification reporting only. | The final message after a decision must state its class and approval source.
C X34 EX DEP-010,DEP-004,API-010 | MUST | Register FluentValidation validators with FluentValidation.DependencyInjectionExtensions and do not add deprecated/legacy ASP.NET automatic-validation integration packages. | Backend validation setup | Follow the project's established invocation pattern instead of adding a competing one. | DEP-010 makes FluentValidation the standard library but prescribes no registration package; no oracle policy forbids the ASP.NET automatic-validation integration packages outright (DEP-004 only requires a deprecation check). | Adding FluentValidation.AspNetCore auto-validation is prohibited; validators are registered through the DI extensions package.
C X35 EX RUN-021,ARC-017 | MUST NOT | Never cache tracked persistence entities across Unit of Work boundaries. | Caching of persistence data | none stated | RUN-021 governs invalidation consistency and ARC-017 the shared scoped context, but no oracle policy forbids caching tracked entities across Unit of Work scopes. | A cache storing EF-tracked entity instances for reuse by later requests is prohibited.
C R180 OL API-007,RUN-018 | MUST | Catch exceptions only to recover, translate into a meaningful abstraction-level outcome, perform necessary cleanup, add context while preserving the original, apply a defined fallback or infrastructure retry; otherwise let them propagate; never swallow external-service failures or catch Exception merely to continue. | Backend exception handling | none stated
C R181 OL ARC-020,API-009 | REQUIRES_APPROVAL | A Result<T> pattern is optional: consider it when an operation has several expected failure modes and it will be used consistently; avoid it where null/bool/simple typed responses suffice; project-wide adoption is a Reserved decision (CLAUDE.md decision control). | Backend outcome modeling | none stated
C R182 OL SEC-016,GOV-013 | MUST | Choose the authentication scheme by the actual application (Bearer/OIDC for SPA->API, cookies for server-rendered apps, Identity when the app owns local users, external OIDC/OAuth when identity is delegated); a business entity named User or profile management does not justify Identity, and do not issue custom JWTs when an existing identity provider owns token issuance. | Backend authentication design | none stated
C R183 OL ARC-020 | REQUIRES_APPROVAL | A new authentication/authorization architecture is a Reserved decision. | Backend authentication | none stated
C R184 OL WF-015,GOV-014 | MUST | Build-fix restores the affected scope to green without hiding failures, weakening behavior or introducing unrelated changes. | Build/test repair | none stated
C R185 OL WF-013 | MUST | Reproduce and diagnose the actual failure (narrowest reproducing command, complete output, errors grouped by root cause) before editing; never guess at a fix first; fix root causes before downstream symptoms. | Build/test repair | none stated
C R186 OL WF-014,WF-008 | MUST | Repair in small bounded iterations: smallest correct change, rerun the same narrowest failing check and compare failure sets, expand verification only after the focused failure is resolved; stop and reassess when progress stalls (same root failure survives different fixes, growing unrelated failures, incompatible toolchain, unavailable infrastructure) and never repeat variants of the same failed guess. | Build/test repair | none stated
C R187 OL WF-015 | MUST NOT | Do not weaken, delete, skip or suppress relevant tests, compiler, TypeScript, lint or analyzer failures merely to pass; understand a diagnostic before changing how it is reported; never turn a strong assertion into a weak one or delete/skip a failing test because fixing it is inconvenient. | Build/test repair | none stated
C R188 OL GOV-014,RUN-009,ARC-001 | MUST | Preserve architecture and project conventions while fixing; a green build obtained by violating them is not a successful repair. | Build/test repair | none stated
C R189 OL WF-005,WF-007 | MUST | Determine the affected scope and do not run unrelated build systems merely because they exist (backend-only failure: the affected .NET project; frontend failure: the workspace; API contract consumed by the frontend: both; documentation-only: no application builds without a reason). | Build/test repair | none stated
C R190 OL WF-018 | MUST | For the frontend, inspect package.json and use scripts that actually exist; never invent script names or assume a test runner. | Frontend build/test commands (local and CI) | Applies the CI-scoped WF-018 rule to local repair as well.
C R191 OL ARC-001,ARC-008,ARC-011,SEC-005,FE-003,WF-015,DEP-014,DEP-015 | MUST NOT | Do not use invalid repair shortcuts: invalid project references (e.g. Application -> Infrastructure), leaking persistence types into Application, business logic in controllers, frontend-only authorization, scattering HTTP calls into components; frontend: converting to any to silence the compiler, disabling strict mode, suppressing template diagnostics without understanding, replacing framework APIs speculatively, adding another state-management or HTTP library to bypass a local issue. | Build/test repair | none stated
C R192 OL WF-016,WF-013 | MUST | For full-stack/contract failures determine the authoritative contract; never patch both sides independently into inconsistent shapes; with OpenAPI generation verify the backend contract, regenerate the client through the normal workflow, review generated changes, then fix handwritten frontend code. | Cross-stack contract failures | none stated
C R193 OL GOV-014,WF-015 | MUST | If expected behavior intentionally changed, update the test to express the new contract and confirm the change was actually requested. | Test failures during repair | none stated
C R194 OL DEP-002,DEP-006,DEP-007 | MUST NOT | Never replace a package or add another merely to get a green build; never duplicate conflicting versions in .csproj files (Central Package Management); never delete package-lock.json reflexively. | Dependency-related build failures | none stated
C R195 OL GOV-014,DEP-008,WF-009,WF-013 | MUST | For upgrade fallout update obsolete APIs deliberately from verified documentation (never fabricate replacement APIs from memory) without unrelated upgrades; never change the declared SDK, Node, framework or language version merely because the local machine differs, and report an unavailable toolchain as a limitation instead of editing source to compensate. | Framework/toolchain failures | none stated
C R196 OL WF-016 | DEFAULT | When generated output is wrong or its source contract/configuration changed, identify the generator and input, fix the authoritative source and regenerate (OpenAPI clients, source generators, persistence migrations, frontend build output); do not manually patch disposable generated output unless the repository or generation workflow treats it as maintained source. | Generated outputs | Output intentionally maintained as source.
C R197 OL GOV-014,WF-008 | MUST | If a fix introduces new failures, determine whether they are a legitimate dependency of the change or revert/correct the fix; never leave the repository knowingly worse than where the repair started; do not clean up unrelated pre-existing failures unless they block verification or the user asks. | Build/test repair | Blocking or user-requested cleanup.
C R198 OL TST-010 | MUST | A behavioral test or probe used to establish correct behavior during diagnosis is preserved in maintained tests (verify step 4) before temporary diagnostics are removed. | Build/test repair | Equivalent coverage exists (per verify).
C R199 OL WF-005,WF-007,WF-003 | MUST | A repair is complete when the affected scope is green (relevant builds and affected tests pass, cross-stack contracts consistent, no known new regression, no accidental workaround in the diff), without requiring unrelated builds; then run verify for the final completion pass when appropriate. | Build/test repair completion | none stated
C R200 OL WF-009 | MUST | Report repairs with backend build, frontend build and tests as PASS / NOT RUN / NOT APPLICABLE plus root cause, changes and remaining issues; never claim the problem is fixed while a known relevant failure remains. | Build/test repair report | none stated
C R201 OL ARC-020,RUN-007 | REQUIRES_APPROVAL | Adding a distributed cache (multi-instance shared cache, HybridCache L2, Redis) is a Reserved infrastructure decision, added only when deployment requirements justify it. | Backend caching | none stated
C R202 OL ARC-016,ARC-024,RUN-018 | MUST | With caching, Application Services keep their normal repository/Unit of Work workflow and coordinate required invalidation through an abstraction rather than bypassing repositories or IUnitOfWork; decide explicitly whether a cache failure falls back to the source, fails the request or returns stale data. | Backend caching | none stated
C R203 OL WF-018 | MUST | Add frontend unit-test and lint CI steps only when the workspace's package.json defines those scripts. | Frontend CI | none stated
C R204 OL TST-004,GOV-013 | MUST | Provide CI integration-test services (database, cache, broker) only when tests require them, isolated from Development and Production data; do not hardcode a database provider into a generic pipeline. | CI test infrastructure | none stated
C R205 OL DEP-018 | MUST NOT | Build and publish container images in CI only when container deployment is part of the project; do not assume container deployment. | CI artifacts | none stated
C R206 OL WF-019 | MUST | Generate database migration scripts/artifacts in CI and execute them at a controlled deployment step with approved credentials, failing visibly on error. | Deployment of database migrations | none stated
C R207 OL WF-010 | MUST | The review is independent and read-only: it reports findings and does not edit files or rewrite working code unless asked; this workflow is the canonical review procedure; verify, not code review, performs the final build/test/diff verification. | Code review | Edits when explicitly asked.
C R208 OL WF-011 | MUST | Prioritize findings: correctness; security and authorization; data integrity and persistence; architectural boundary violations; concurrency and integration; missing meaningful tests; maintainability; credible performance; never elevate style above behavioral defects and do not report formatting owned by tooling. | Code review | none stated
C R209 OL WF-001,WF-010 | MUST | Establish the review scope and read enough surrounding code (callers, contracts, dependencies) and the governing standards and relevant knowledge to understand changed behavior; never review isolated lines; check architecture against the loaded owners rather than a private checklist. | Code review | none stated
C R210 OL WF-012,WF-011 | MUST NOT | Do not introduce or enforce patterns the repository has not adopted (differing from an unadopted pattern is not a finding); report only correctness failures with a plausible execution path. | Code review | none stated
C R211 OL GOV-003,GOV-006,GOV-001,GOV-005 | MUST | Review specification conformance in both directions: required behavior is not missing or weakened and no unapproved behavior is introduced (new lengths/limits, reserved names/values, observable string-comparison semantics incl. database configuration or framework defaults, new required fields, extra workflow restrictions, additional user-visible validation); approved synchronized details are not drift; internal implementation details are not product behavior. | Code review with non-empty SPECIFICATION.md / CUSTOM_SETTINGS.md | Approved and synchronized details.
C R212 OL WF-011,GOV-012,PST-008,GOV-017 | MUST | Architecture review checks role/dependency rules, repository/persistence boundary, proportionality (consequential patterns only with approved need) and, when the persistence model changed, that persistence-change steps including the development seeder were followed; frontend code is checked against frontend rules without mechanically applying backend project-boundary rules. | Code review | none stated
C R213 OL WF-007,WF-011 | MUST | Review data and security aspects (commit behavior, destructive changes, raw SQL parameterization, authorization, trust boundaries, secrets, sensitive logging, concurrency, migrations, cache consistency); run the full security-scan only when an audit was requested, the change is materially security-sensitive, or a concrete finding justifies it. | Code review | none stated
C R214 OL RUN-016,WF-011 | MUST | Review external calls for cancellation, timeouts, idempotency, retry safety, failure translation and lifetime; never demand retries for operations that are not safe to retry. | Code review | none stated
C R215 OL TST-009,TST-010,TST-011,WF-011 | MUST | Check that changed behavior has useful maintained coverage; for bug fixes look for a regression test when recurrence is plausible; behavioral probes must be preserved as maintained tests; do not request duplicate tests when equivalent coverage exists. | Code review | Equivalent coverage exists.
C R216 OL WF-011,RUN-008 | MUST | Report performance only for credible reasons (N+1, unbounded materialization, blocking async I/O, repeated expensive remote calls, demonstrated hot paths); do not recommend speculative optimizations. | Code review | none stated
C R217 OL WF-021,WF-020 | MUST NOT | Review must not require an MCP server or specific analyzer; tool output is evidence, not authority — read the code before turning a warning into a finding. | Code review (applies the security-review tool-independence rule to review) | none stated
C R218 OL WF-011 | MUST | Report findings ordered by severity (Critical, High, Medium, Low) with location, what is wrong, why it matters and the smallest reasonable fix. | Code review output | none stated
C R219 OL WF-012 | MUST NOT | Never invent findings to populate a category; when there are no meaningful findings say so and name important areas that could not be verified; no filler praise. | Code review output | none stated

